Found while fixing #32. CryptX returns decrypted plaintext in an SV whose PV
buffer is not NUL-terminated at SvCUR, violating the Perl PV invariant.

Measured on CryptX 0.087, perl 5.36.0, x86_64 glibc. Affects at least
Crypt::AuthEnc::GCM::decrypt_add and the functional gcm_decrypt_verify built on
it; the same allocation pattern very likely affects the other AuthEnc modes and
anything else in CryptX that sets a PV by length without terminating. NOT yet
surveyed beyond GCM -- that survey is part of this ticket.

Why it matters: Perl_sv_2nv falls through to Atof(SvPVX), a C string read with
no length, for every value grok_number cannot settle as an integer inside a UV.
So a numeric read of a CryptX-returned scalar can pick up heap bytes past the
plaintext. Any consumer doing arithmetic on decrypted output is exposed. In
File::SOPS this returned a stored 1e20 as 1e21 on ~4% of leaves.

Minimal reproduction (no File::SOPS):

  use Crypt::AuthEnc::GCM qw(gcm_encrypt_authenticate gcm_decrypt_verify);
  # peek the returned SV via @_ aliasing so nothing copies it, read the byte at
  # SvPVX[SvCUR] with unpack P, compare $_[0] + 0.0 against ("$_[0]") + 0.0.
  # Devel::Peek shows CUR=21 LEN=23 and no trailing \0 where a Perl-made string
  # of the same shape shows one.
  # Working script: scratchpad cryptx_poison.pl / candidates.pl from the #32
  # session; regenerate from the ticket notes if the scratchpad is gone.

Our workaround is in File::SOPS::Encrypted::decrypt_bytes and does not depend on
an upstream fix, so this is not blocking. Do it anyway: every other CPAN consumer
of CryptX has the same exposure and no reason to suspect it.

Do NOT open anything on the public GitHub tracker of this distribution for it --
this belongs upstream at DCIT/perl-CryptX, and only on the maintainer's say-so.
